.TH GOZER 1 "October 2026" "tt\-gozer 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gozer \- cooperative Tenstorrent chip leasing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gozer
[\fB\-\-version\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gozer
lets several agents and people share one box with Tenstorrent accelerators. A workload takes a lease on a board before it opens /dev/tenstorrent/*, and releases it afterwards. A lease is checked against the open file descriptors of real processes, so a crashed workload does not hold a board for ever. On a board that holds two chips or fewer the lease covers the whole board, so asking for one chip may grant two.
.PP
Every command accepts
.B \-\-json
for machine\-readable output.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status [--json]
Show who holds what and who is waiting. Read\-only: it never reaps or changes state.
.TP
.B topology [--json]
Show the chips, the boards they sit on, and the lease grain.
.TP
.B acquire|summon --chips N|all|LO\-HI --who WHO [--reason R] [--exact X] [--fresh] [--expect D] [--ticket T] [--no\-queue] [--owner\-pid PID]
Take a lease on N chips, or queue a ticket if none are free (exit 10). The lease is judged by open file descriptors: a standalone lease has a 900 second grace window, and --owner\-pid makes a supervisor's pid own it instead. --fresh requires chips a real reset marked clean. --no\-queue exits at once when no chips are free, without taking a ticket.
.TP
.B wait TICKET [--timeout D]
Block until a queued ticket is granted. Bounded (8 minutes by default) so a loop is safe.
.TP
.B queue [--json]
List waiting requests.
.TP
.B cancel TICKET
Drop a queue ticket.
.TP
.B renew LEASE [--expect D]
Restart a detached lease's grace window. --expect also extends the advisory deadline.
.TP
.B release|banish LEASE [--no\-reset] [--force]
Give the chips back and reset them with tt\-smi. A reset opens every device on the box for about 42 seconds.
.TP
.B reset LEASE [--force]
Reset the lease's chips in place and keep the lease. Refuses while one of your own processes holds a device open.
.TP
.B reset\-all --who WHO [--reason R]
Host\-wide tt\-smi \-r all under a lease on every unit. It never queues: it refuses unless the whole box is free.
.TP
.B reconcile [--sudo]
Re\-derive state from kernel truth and reap leases whose owner is gone. --sudo also checks other users' processes.
.TP
.B adopt TARGET --who WHO [--reason R]
Wrap a lease around untracked running work.
.TP
.B env LEASE
Print the export lines for a lease.
.TP
.B run [--chips N] [--who W] [--reason R] [--exact X] [--fresh] [--expect D] \-\- COMMAND...
Acquire, run COMMAND, and always release. The release waits up to 10 seconds for the device to close.
.TP
.B history [\-n N] [--json]
Show who held what, when, and for how long. The last 20 events by default.
.TP
.B report [--out PATH] [--json]
Regenerate the containment\-log page from history.jsonl. Inside a git checkout the default output is docs/log.html at the repository root; anywhere else --out PATH is required. Missing parent directories are created. Never touches hardware.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
granted / success
.TP
.B 10
queued \(em a ticket is on stdout
.TP
.B 11
wait timed out, still queued
.TP
.B 12
unavailable and queueing disabled
.TP
.B 13
no such lease or ticket
.TP
.B 14
topology unreadable
.TP
.B 15
release or reset refused, nothing done \(em a device is still open, or the lease's units now belong to someone else; for reset also a lease record that disagrees with its units, or has no units or chips, or a malformed chip id
.TP
.B 16
the allocator mutex is stuck; the message names the path to clear
.TP
.B 17
reset ran tt\-smi and it failed; the lease is still held
.TP
.B 18
reset ran, and afterwards the lease was gone or another lease held its chips; the reset may have hit chips that were no longer yours
.TP
.B 19
release tore the lease down but its reset failed; the units are not marked clean (so \-\-fresh refuses them) and the chips may be unreadable until reset. The output, and recovery in \-\-json, give the commands. Before 0.3.5 this exited 0
.TP
.B 20
reset\-all ran tt\-smi and it succeeded, but some chips still read all ones; gozer has nothing further to try, so a human must power\-cycle the box or reseat the card
.TP
.B 130
gozer run was interrupted by a signal (128 + SIGINT); the lease was still released
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $GOZER_ROOT
Lease state: gate, leases, queue and mutex directories. Default /tmp/tt\-gozer. It is lost on reboot on purpose, because a reboot resets the chips.
.TP
.I $GOZER_HISTORY_ROOT/history.jsonl
Append\-only log of every grant, release, reap and refusal. Default ~/.local/state/tt\-gozer (or $XDG_STATE_HOME/tt\-gozer).
.TP
.I /usr/share/tt\-gozer/skills
The Claude Code skills. gozer\-install\-skills links them into ~/.claude/skills.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/gozer\-reconcile.{service,timer}
Optional user units that run gozer reconcile every five minutes. The package never enables them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GOZER_ROOT
Directory for lease state.
.TP
.B GOZER_HISTORY_ROOT
Directory for history.jsonl.
.TP
.B GOZER_SYSFS_ROOT
Where to read the chips from. Default /sys/class/tenstorrent. Meant for tests.
.TP
.B GOZER_PROC_ROOT
Where to scan for open devices. Default /proc. Meant for tests.
.TP
.B GOZER_RESET_CMD
The reset program. Default tt\-smi.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tt\-smi (1),
.BR gozer\-install\-skills (1)
.PP
https://github.com/tsingletaryTT/tt\-gozer
